---
import { readFileSync } from "node:fs";
import { join } from "node:path";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { ROOT } from "../lib/spec";

const prompt = readFileSync(join(ROOT, "kit", "prompt.md"), "utf8").trim();
const REPO = "https://github.com/OpenBriefing/openbriefing";
---

<Base title="Add your tags" description="Draft your summary with the assistant you already use, review it, and put Open Briefing Standard tags on your site: Ghost, a static site, plain HTML or a feed." path="/publish">
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Add your tags</h1>
    <p class="lede">Draft with the assistant you already use, read every line, and paste. A first post takes a few minutes.</p>

    <h2 id="draft">1. Draft</h2>
    <p>
      Copy this prompt into your assistant, then paste your post after it. It returns a briefing file: a headline, a
      summary of 200 to 350 words, the lines to keep and a few passages worth quoting.
    </p>
    <details class="prompt">
      <summary>Read the prompt</summary>
      <pre class="wrap"><code id="prompt-text">{prompt}</code></pre>
    </details>
    <p><button type="button" id="prompt-copy">Copy the prompt</button></p>
    <p class="note">
      The prompt is in the public domain. Change it to suit your voice. If your assistant loads skills, the same
      instructions are packaged as{" "}<a href={`${REPO}/tree/main/kit/skill`}>a skill</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="review">2. Read it, then build</h2>
    <p>
      Paste the draft into{" "}<a href="/build">the tag builder</a>. It checks the lengths, looks each highlight up in your post,
      shows the card a tool could make and reads the summary aloud.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then read every line as if someone were quoting you, because they will be. A tool presents these words as yours, and
      a summary cannot be recalled once it has been used. What to look for:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>The first two sentences stand on their own. A tool that needs something shorter often uses just those.</li>
      <li>It says what the post says: no added background, no conclusion you did not draw.</li>
      <li>It reads well aloud: no "see below", no web addresses, abbreviations spelled out once.</li>
      <li>Each highlight is a passage you would be glad to see quoted alone, copied word for word.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>When it says what you mean, mark it approved and copy the tags.</p>

    <h2 id="place">3. Put the tags on your site</h2>

    <h3 id="ghost">Ghost</h3>
    <p>
      Open the post, then its settings, then <strong>Code injection</strong>. Paste the builder's Ghost output into the
      post header box and update the post. Nothing else about the post changes.
    </p>
    <p>For a whole catalogue, the command line keeps one briefing file per post and writes the approved ones for you:</p>
    <pre><code>export GHOST_URL=https://your-site.example
export GHOST_ADMIN_API_KEY=…

npx openbriefing ghost pull posts/
npx openbriefing ghost push posts/ --dry-run
npx openbriefing ghost push posts/</code></pre>
    <p>The key is a custom integration's Admin API key: in Ghost Admin, open Integrations and add a custom integration.</p>
    <p>
      <code>pull</code> writes each post's text with an empty draft above it, for you or your assistant to fill in. It
      never changes Ghost and never overwrites a file. <code>push</code> writes only files marked{" "}<code>status: approved</code>, only between its two marker comments in the post header, and skips a post whose
      tags have not changed. Keep the folder out of any public repository: it holds your posts.
    </p>

    <h4 id="ghost-hook">Or have the tags written when you publish</h4>
    <p>
      A small hook can draft the tags for you each time a post goes live, and you edit them afterwards if you want to.
      Run it anywhere that can run Node and has a public address:
    </p>
    <pre><code>export GHOST_URL=https://your-site.example
export GHOST_ADMIN_API_KEY=…
export GHOST_WEBHOOK_SECRET=…        # any long random text
export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=…           # your own Anthropic key
export OBS_HOOK_URL=https://your-hook.example   # where Ghost can reach it

npx openbriefing ghost hook</code></pre>
    <p>
      On start it registers two webhooks on your integration, for a post being published and a published post being
      updated. About a minute after you publish, the post's Code injection header holds the tags. To change them, edit
      them there: once you have, the tags are yours and the hook never writes to that post again. Delete the block and
      the next update drafts a fresh one. <code>npx openbriefing ghost draft</code> does the same for posts you published
      before.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Anthropic key is your own, from your Anthropic account. The hook runs on your server and calls Claude with it,
      one call per post, billed to you. Nothing passes through this site, and the hook will not start without the key.
    </p>
    <p>
      Know what you are choosing: these tags go out as your words before you have read them, and a tool that has used a
      summary keeps it. The hook quotes only passages it finds in the post, leaves members-only and very short posts
      alone, and never touches tags you wrote. If you would rather read first, use <code>pull</code> and{" "}<code>push</code> above.
    </p>

    <h3 id="static">A static site</h3>
    <p>
      Put the builder's front matter output in the post, under an <code>obs:</code> key, and have your head template write
      one tag per key. Or write the tags at build time from the same file:
    </p>
    <pre><code>npx openbriefing tags content/a-post.md</code></pre>
    <p>
      The command reads the <code>obs:</code> key from a post's front matter, checks each highlight against the post's
      text, and exits with an error for a draft, so a build cannot publish one by accident.
    </p>

    <h3 id="html">Any page you can edit</h3>
    <p>Paste the builder's HTML output inside the page's <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>.</p>

    <h3 id="feeds">Your feed</h3>
    <p>
      The same tags can ride in an RSS or Atom feed, so a feed reader can use them without fetching each page. Each tag
      becomes an element, and a sub-property becomes an attribute: <code>obs:summary:short</code> is{" "}<code>&lt;obs:summary length="short"&gt;</code>. The builder's feed output, or{" "}<code>openbriefing tags --format feed</code>, writes them. <a href="/spec/v1#14-feeds">Section 14 of the spec</a>{" "}has
      the rules.
    </p>

    <h3 id="hosted">Where it cannot be done yet</h3>
    <p>
      A platform that gives you no way to add tags to a page's head, such as Substack, Medium or LinkedIn, cannot carry
      them. WordPress will get a plugin.
    </p>

    <h2 id="check">4. Check the live page</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/check">Check the page</a>{" "}once it is published, or check everything at once:
    </p>
    <pre><code>npx openbriefing check https://your-site.example/sitemap.xml --sitemap</code></pre>
    <p>
      Then, if you like, <a href="/adopters">add your site to the list</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>

<script>
  const button = document.getElementById("prompt-copy") as HTMLButtonElement;
  const text = document.getElementById("prompt-text")!;
  button.addEventListener("click", async () => {
    try {
      await navigator.clipboard.writeText(text.textContent ?? "");
      button.textContent = "Copied";
    } catch {
      (text.closest("details") as HTMLDetailsElement).open = true;
      button.textContent = "Select the text above and copy it";
    }
    setTimeout(() => (button.textContent = "Copy the prompt"), 2500);
  });
</script>

<style>
  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2.2rem, 1.4rem + 3.4vw, 3.6rem);
  }
  .prompt > summary {
    cursor: pointer;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .prompt pre {
    margin-top: 0.8rem;
    max-height: 28rem;
    overflow-y: auto;
  }
</style>
